## Percentiles and Boundary Detection

`percentile` takes a list and a percentile (0-100) and returns the value at that boundary. This is essential for understanding distributions: p50 is the median, p95 tells you the high-water mark for most of your data, p99 catches the outliers. We define it as a local helper using linear interpolation between the two closest ranks: sort ascending, compute the fractional position `p/100 * (n-1)`, and blend the two neighbouring values. That is the method that makes p50 come out as the true median—for an even-length list it averages the two middle values instead of arbitrarily picking one.

```scheme
;; Local helpers used throughout this chapter.

;; percentile: linear interpolation between closest ranks (the R-7 method, what
;; most spreadsheets and stats packages mean by \"percentile\"). Sort ascending,
;; land at position p/100 * (n-1), and interpolate between the two neighbours.
;; This is what makes p50 the true median of an even-length list — a rounded
;; index would pick one of the two middle values instead of averaging them.
;; The rank arithmetic is kept in whole numbers until the last divide, so
;; p90 of a ten-point series comes back 36.5 and not 36.49999999999999.
(define (percentile xs p)
  (let* ((sorted (sort xs <))
         (n (length sorted))
         (num (* p (- n 1)))
         (lo (inexact->exact (floor (/ num 100))))
         (rem (- num (* lo 100))))
    (if (or (= rem 0) (>= (+ lo 1) n))
        (list-ref sorted lo)
        (+ (list-ref sorted lo)
           (/ (* rem (- (list-ref sorted (+ lo 1)) (list-ref sorted lo))) 100)))))

;; pearson-correlation: Pearson r for two equal-length lists.
(define (pearson-correlation xs ys)
  (let* ((n (length xs))
         (mx (/ (apply + xs) n))
         (my (/ (apply + ys) n))
         (num (apply + (map (lambda (x y) (* (- x mx) (- y my))) xs ys)))
         (sxx (apply + (map (lambda (x) (expt (- x mx) 2)) xs)))
         (syy (apply + (map (lambda (y) (expt (- y my) 2)) ys))))
    ;; One sqrt of the product, not a product of two sqrts: multiplying two
    ;; rounded roots loses a bit, and a perfect correlation comes back as
    ;; 0.9999999999999998 instead of 1.
    (if (or (= sxx 0) (= syy 0))
        'nan
        (/ num (sqrt (* sxx syy))))))

;; haversine: great-circle distance in km between two lat/lng points.
(define (haversine lat1 lng1 lat2 lng2)
  (let* ((r 6371)
         (to-rad (lambda (d) (* d (/ (* 4 (atan 1)) 180))))
         (dlat (to-rad (- lat2 lat1)))
         (dlng (to-rad (- lng2 lng1)))
         (a (+ (expt (sin (/ dlat 2)) 2)
               (* (cos (to-rad lat1))
                  (cos (to-rad lat2))
                  (expt (sin (/ dlng 2)) 2))))
         (c (* 2 (atan (sqrt a) (sqrt (- 1 a))))))
    (* r c)))

;; velocity: mean rate of change (mean of consecutive differences).
(define (velocity series)
  (if (< (length series) 2)
      'nan
      (let ((diffs (let loop ((rem (cdr series)) (prev (car series)) (acc '()))
                     (if (null? rem)
                         (reverse acc)
                         (loop (cdr rem) (car rem) (cons (- (car rem) prev) acc))))))
        (/ (apply + diffs) (length diffs)))))

;; linear-trend: fit y = a + b*x by least squares, return (list slope intercept).
(define (linear-trend series)
  (let* ((n (length series))
         (xs (iota n 0))
         (mx (/ (apply + xs) n))
         (my (/ (apply + series) n))
         (num (apply + (map (lambda (x y) (* (- x mx) (- y my))) xs series)))
         (den (apply + (map (lambda (x) (expt (- x mx) 2)) xs)))
         (slope (if (= den 0) 0 (/ num den)))
         (intercept (- my (* slope mx))))
    (list slope intercept)))

;; autocorr: autocorrelation of series at given lag.
(define (autocorr series lag)
  (let* ((n (length series))
         (mean (/ (apply + series) n))
         (centered (map (lambda (x) (- x mean)) series))
         (variance (/ (apply + (map (lambda (x) (* x x)) centered)) n)))
    (if (= variance 0)
        'nan
        (let* ((pairs-n (- n lag))
               (num (/ (apply + (map (lambda (i)
                                       (* (list-ref centered i)
                                          (list-ref centered (+ i lag))))
                                     (iota pairs-n 0)))
                       pairs-n)))
          (/ num variance)))))
```

```scheme
;; Find the median (p50) of a list of response times.
(define (median-response times)
  (if (null? times)
      'nan
      (percentile times 50)))

(median-response '(12 15 18 22 29 35 47))
;; => 22

(median-response '())
;; => nan
```

Guard, call, done. Now a program that computes *three* percentiles—p25, p50, p75—and returns them as a summary:

```scheme
;; Compute quartiles (p25, p50, p75) for a list of values.
;; Returns (list p25 p50 p75), or (list 'nan 'nan 'nan) if empty.
(define (quartiles values)
  (if (null? values)
      (list 'nan 'nan 'nan)
      (list (percentile values 25)
            (percentile values 50)
            (percentile values 75))))

(quartiles '(5 7 9 11 13 15 17 19 21))
;; => (9 13 17)

(quartiles '())
;; => (nan nan nan)
```

Three calls to the same verb, each with a different argument. The guard is shared. This is composition at the simplest level: apply the same operation to the same data with different parameters, collect the results.

