## Percentile Bands and Anomaly Detection

A common pattern: compute several percentiles, then check if a new value falls outside a band (e.g., above p95 or below p5). This is the foundation of simple anomaly detection.

Here's a program that computes p5 and p95 for a baseline series, then checks if a new value is an outlier:

```scheme
;; Check if a new value is an outlier (outside p5-p95 band) relative to baseline.
;; Returns 'high, 'low, or 'normal.
(define (outlier-check baseline value)
  (if (< (length baseline) 10)
      'insufficient-data
      (let ((p5 (percentile baseline 5))
            (p95 (percentile baseline 95)))
        (cond
         ((> value p95) 'high)
         ((< value p5) 'low)
         (else 'normal)))))

(outlier-check '(10 12 14 15 16 18 20 22 24 26 28 30) 35)
;; => high

(outlier-check '(10 12 14 15 16 18 20 22 24 26 28 30) 20)
;; => normal

(outlier-check '(10 12 14 15 16 18 20 22 24 26 28 30) 11)
;; => low  ;; surprising? the band is narrower than the data — see below

(outlier-check '(10 12 14) 50)
;; => insufficient-data
```

We require at least 10 baseline points (a heuristic—p5 and p95 are meaningless with tiny samples). Compute the band, classify the new value. This is a *decision route*: data in, symbol out.

The third call is the one to sit with. The baseline runs from 10 to 30, and 11 is comfortably inside that range—yet it classifies as `low`. The reason is that p5 and p95 are *not* the minimum and maximum. For these twelve points the band is `[11.1, 28.9]`, computed by interpolating between the first two and the last two values. So 11 falls below the band and 29 would fall above it, even though both are values you could plausibly have drawn from the baseline itself.

That is not a bug; it is what a 5%–95% band means. With only twelve points, 5% of the data is 0.6 of a point, so the band has to cut somewhere inside the gap between the two lowest observations. The practical lesson: a p5/p95 band on a small baseline is *tight*, and it will flag values that a human eyeballing the range would call ordinary. If you want a band that only fires on genuine excursions, either collect more baseline or widen the percentiles.

Now a program that processes a *stream* of new values and classifies each one:

```scheme
;; Classify each value in a stream as 'high, 'low, or 'normal relative to baseline.
(define (classify-stream baseline stream)
  (if (< (length baseline) 10)
      (map (lambda (x) 'insufficient-data) stream)
      (let ((p5 (percentile baseline 5))
            (p95 (percentile baseline 95)))
        (map (lambda (value)
               (cond
                ((> value p95) 'high)
                ((< value p5) 'low)
                (else 'normal)))
             stream))))

(classify-stream
 '(10 12 14 15 16 18 20 22 24 26 28 30)
 '(35 11 20 29 8 50))
;; => (high low normal high low high)  ;; band is [11.1, 28.9]
```

We compute the percentile band once, then map the classification over the stream. Each element is independent. The program is efficient (one computation of the band) and clear.

