## Multi-Verb Orchestration: Stats Summary Route

Now we compose several verbs into a single route that takes a list of numbers and returns a structured summary: count, mean, p50, p95, and velocity. Each stage can fail; we check and escalate cleanly.

```scheme
;; Compute a comprehensive stats summary for a numeric series.
;; Returns an association list with keys: count mean p50 p95 velocity,
;; or (escalate \"insufficient-data\" detail) if series is too short.
(define (stats-summary series)
  (cond
   ((null? series)
    (list 'escalate \"empty-series\" \"cannot compute stats on empty list\"))
   ((< (length series) 5)
    (list 'escalate \"insufficient-data\" \"need at least 5 points for summary\"))
   (else
    (let* ((count (length series))
           (total (apply + series))
           (mean (/ total count))
           (p50 (percentile series 50))
           (p95 (percentile series 95))
           (vel (velocity series)))
      (list (cons 'count count)
            (cons 'mean mean)
            (cons 'p50 p50)
            (cons 'p95 p95)
            (cons 'velocity vel))))))

(stats-summary '(10 12 15 18 22 28 35 42 50))
;; => ((count . 9)
;;     (mean . 25.777...)
;;     (p50 . 22)
;;     (p95 . 50)
;;     (velocity . 5.0))

(stats-summary '(10 12))
;; => (escalate \"insufficient-data\" \"need at least 5 points for summary\")

(stats-summary '())
;; => (escalate \"empty-series\" \"cannot compute stats on empty list\")
```

This is a *route*: data flows through a series of verbs, and the result is a structured summary. The guards at the top are ESCAPE—if the input can't support the computation, we escalate immediately with a reason. The body is FORWARD—each stage computes a piece of the answer, and we assemble them into an alist.

Now a program that computes summaries for *multiple* series and returns a list of summaries:

```scheme
;; Compute stats summaries for multiple series, returning a list of results.
;; Each result is either a summary alist or an escalate form.
(define (batch-summaries series-list)
  (map stats-summary series-list))

(batch-summaries 
 '((10 12 15 18 22 28 35 42 50)
   (5 5 5 5 5)
   (100 110 105 115 120 125 130)))
;; => (((count . 9) (mean . 25.777...) ...)
;;     ((count . 5) (mean . 5) ...)
;;     ((count . 7) (mean . 115) ...))
```

We map the route over a list of series. Each series gets its own summary. If any series is too short, its result will be an escalate form. The caller sees the honest shape of the results.

