## Clamping, Rounding, and Boundary Math

`math/clamp` constrains a value to a range. `math/round` rounds to a specified precision. These are essential for post-processing computed values—ensuring outputs stay within valid bounds.

```scheme
;; Clamp a computed percentage to 0-100 range and round to 1 decimal place.
(define (clamp-percent raw-pct)
  (math/round (math/clamp raw-pct 0 100) 1))

(clamp-percent 42.6789)
;; => 42.7

(clamp-percent -5)
;; => 0

(clamp-percent 105)
;; => 100
```

Two verbs, chained. The output of `math/clamp` feeds into `math/round`. This is the simplest form of FORWARD composition.

Now a program that computes a margin percentage, clamps it to 0-100, and classifies it as 'low, 'medium, or 'high:

```scheme
;; Compute margin percentage, clamp it, and classify.
(define (margin-status cost revenue)
  (if (<= revenue 0)
      (list 'margin 'nan 'classification 'invalid)
      (let* ((raw-margin (math/pct (- revenue cost) revenue))
             (clamped (math/clamp raw-margin 0 100))
             (rounded (math/round clamped 1))
             (class (cond
                     ((< rounded 10) 'low)
                     ((< rounded 30) 'medium)
                     (else 'high))))
        (list 'margin rounded 'classification class))))

(margin-status 80 100)
;; => (margin 20 classification medium)

(margin-status 70 100)
;; => (margin 30 classification high)  ;; exactly 30 is NOT medium — see below

(margin-status 10 100)
;; => (margin 90 classification high)

(margin-status 100 50)
;; => (margin 0 classification low)  ;; negative margin clamped to 0

(margin-status 50 0)
;; => (margin nan classification invalid)
```

Three verbs, one decision ladder, result as an alist. The guard at the top prevents division by zero. The clamp ensures the margin stays in 0-100 even if cost exceeds revenue. The classification is a simple threshold check.

Read that second call carefully, because it is the kind of thing that costs you an afternoon. A margin of exactly 30 comes back `high`, not `medium`. The ladder tests `(< rounded 30)`, so the bands it actually carves out are `[0, 10)` low, `[10, 30)` medium, `[30, 100]` high—half-open, each band excluding its upper edge. That is the conventional reading of a `cond` chain built from `<`, and it is self-consistent, but it means every boundary value falls into the band *above* the one you might have said out loud. If you want 30 to read as medium, you have to write `(<= rounded 30)` and say so. Pick a convention, write it in the comment, and test the boundary itself—never just the middle of each band.

