## Haversine Distance and Geo Math

Our `haversine` helper computes the great-circle distance between two lat/lng points (in kilometers). This is essential for geo-based analytics—how far apart are two locations?

```scheme
;; Compute distance between two locations (lat/lng pairs).
(define (distance loc1 loc2)
  (let ((lat1 (car loc1))
        (lng1 (cadr loc1))
        (lat2 (car loc2))
        (lng2 (cadr loc2)))
    (haversine lat1 lng1 lat2 lng2)))

(distance '(40.7128 -74.0060) '(34.0522 -118.2437))
;; => ~3936 km (New York to Los Angeles)
```

Unpack the pairs, call the verb. Now a program that computes the *total* distance along a route (list of waypoints):

```scheme
;; Compute total distance along a route (list of lat/lng pairs).
(define (route-distance waypoints)
  (if (< (length waypoints) 2)
      0
      (let loop ((remaining (cdr waypoints))
                 (prev (car waypoints))
                 (total 0))
        (if (null? remaining)
            total
            (let ((dist (distance prev (car remaining))))
              (loop (cdr remaining)
                    (car remaining)
                    (+ total dist)))))))

(route-distance 
 '((40.7128 -74.0060)   ; New York
   (41.8781 -87.6298)   ; Chicago
   (34.0522 -118.2437)  ; Los Angeles
   ))
;; => 3948.26278092154  (km: 1144.29 New York->Chicago + 2803.97 Chicago->Los Angeles)
```

Named-let recursion, accumulator for total distance. Each segment is computed with `distance`, added to the total, and we move to the next pair. The pattern is identical to `running-sum`, but the operation is haversine instead of addition.

