## Autocorrelation and Lag Analysis

Our `autocorr` helper computes the autocorrelation of a series at a given lag—how correlated is the series with a shifted version of itself. This is useful for detecting periodicity or memory in time series.

```scheme
;; Compute autocorrelation at lag 1 (is each value correlated with the previous one?).
(define (autocorr-lag1 series)
  (if (< (length series) 3)
      'nan
      (autocorr series 1)))

(autocorr-lag1 '(10 12 14 16 18 20))
;; => 0.6  (positive, but nowhere near 1)

(autocorr-lag1 '(10 5 10 5 10 5))
;; => -1  (perfect period-2 oscillation)

(autocorr-lag1 '(10 10))
;; => nan
```

Guard for minimum length (autocorr needs at least lag+2 points).

That first result deserves a second look, because the intuition most people bring to it is wrong. A steady climb `10 12 14 16 18 20` *looks* like the most correlated series imaginable, so you expect lag-1 autocorrelation near 1. You get 0.6. Autocorrelation does not measure \"is this series smooth\"—it measures how well each value predicts the one `lag` steps later *once the mean is subtracted*. Subtracting the mean turns the ramp into `-5 -3 -1 1 3 5`, which changes sign in the middle, and the pairs that straddle that sign change contribute negative terms. Only a series that repeats itself with period `lag` scores near ±1, which is exactly what the oscillating series does: subtract its mean and every value is `+2.5` or `-2.5`, so every lag-1 pair contributes the same negative product and the result is exactly -1. A trend is not a cycle. Autocorrelation is a cycle detector.

Now a program that computes autocorrelation at *multiple* lags and returns a list:

```scheme
;; Compute autocorrelation at lags 1 through max-lag.
(define (autocorr-lags series max-lag)
  (if (< (length series) (+ max-lag 2))
      (map (lambda (lag) 'nan) (iota max-lag 1))
      (map (lambda (lag) (autocorr series lag))
           (iota max-lag 1))))

(autocorr-lags '(10 12 14 16 18 20 22 24) 3)
;; => (5/7 23/63 -1/21)
;;    a linear trend DECAYS as the lag grows, and is near zero by lag 3

(autocorr-lags '(10 5 10 5 10 5 10 5) 2)
;; => (-1 1)  ;; oscillates with period 2: -1 at odd lags, +1 at even lags
```

The contrast between those two lines is the whole lesson. The oscillating series is genuinely periodic, so it pins to exactly -1 and +1 and stays there. The ramp starts moderately positive and falls off a cliff—by lag 3 it is essentially zero. If you scan an autocorrelation profile looking for a repeating pattern, a decaying profile like the first one is telling you there isn't one; it's telling you there's a *trend*, which is `linear-trend`'s job, not `autocorr`'s.

`iota` generates `(1 2 3 ... max-lag)`. We map `autocorr` over those lags. If the series is too short, we return a list of 'nan. (Note: `iota` is a common Scheme helper; if not available in Motoi Scheme, replace with a manual list or named-let.)

