## Multi-Series Comparison: Pairwise Correlations

Given a list of series, compute the correlation between every pair. This is a classic O(n²) operation, but with small n (say, 5 series), it's tractable.

```scheme
;; Compute pairwise correlations between all series in a list.
;; Returns a list of ((i . j) . correlation) for all i < j.
(define (pairwise-correlations series-list)
  (let loop-i ((i 0)
               (rest-i series-list)
               (results '()))
    (if (null? rest-i)
        (reverse results)
        (let loop-j ((j (+ i 1))
                     (rest-j (cdr rest-i))
                     (results-j results))
          (if (null? rest-j)
              (loop-i (+ i 1) (cdr rest-i) results-j)
              (let ((corr (safe-correlation (car rest-i) (car rest-j))))
                (loop-j (+ j 1)
                        (cdr rest-j)
                        (cons (cons (cons i j) corr) results-j))))))))

(pairwise-correlations 
 '((1 2 3 4 5)
   (2 4 6 8 10)
   (5 4 3 2 1)))
;; => (((0 . 1) . 1)
;;     ((0 . 2) . -1)
;;     ((1 . 2) . -1))
```

Nested loops: outer over each series `i`, inner over each series `j > i`. For each pair, compute correlation and cons onto results. The result is a list of `((i . j) . correlation)` pairs. This is *exhaustive pairwise orchestration*—a common pattern in multi-variate stats.

