## Time-Window Aggregates: Rolling Stats

A rolling (or moving) aggregate computes a stat over a sliding window. Here's a program that computes a rolling mean with a window of 3:

```scheme
;; Compute rolling mean with window size 3.
(define (rolling-mean-3 series)
  (if (< (length series) 3)
      '()
      (let loop ((remaining series)
                 (result '()))
        (if (< (length remaining) 3)
            (reverse result)
            (let* ((window (list (car remaining)
                                 (cadr remaining)
                                 (caddr remaining)))
                   (mean (/ (apply + window) 3)))
              (loop (cdr remaining)
                    (cons mean result)))))))

(rolling-mean-3 '(10 12 14 16 18 20 22))
;; => (12 14 16 18 20)
```

Named-let recursion. At each position, extract the next three elements, compute their mean, cons onto result. The output is shorter than the input by window-1.

Now a program that computes a rolling *maximum* with a window of 4:

```scheme
;; Compute rolling maximum with window size 4.
(define (rolling-max-4 series)
  (if (< (length series) 4)
      '()
      (let loop ((remaining series)
                 (result '()))
        (if (< (length remaining) 4)
            (reverse result)
            (let* ((window (list (car remaining)
                                 (cadr remaining)
                                 (caddr remaining)
                                 (cadddr remaining)))
                   (win-max (apply max window)))
              (loop (cdr remaining)
                    (cons win-max result)))))))

(rolling-max-4 '(5 3 8 2 9 1 7 6))
;; => (8 9 9 9 9)
```

Same structure, different operation. This is the *sliding window* pattern—extremely common in time-series work.

