## Median Absolute Deviation (MAD)

MAD is a robust measure of variability: compute the median, then compute the median of absolute deviations from that median. No verb for it, but we can build it.

```scheme
;; Compute median absolute deviation (MAD).
(define (mad series)
  (if (< (length series) 2)
      'nan
      (let* ((median (percentile series 50))
             (deviations (map (lambda (x) (abs (- x median))) series))
             (mad-value (percentile deviations 50)))
        mad-value)))

(mad '(1 2 3 4 5 100))
;; => 1.5  ;; median is 3.5, deviations are [2.5 1.5 0.5 0.5 1.5 96.5], median of those is 1.5
```

Compute median, map to get absolute deviations, compute median of those. This is a *two-stage stat*—output of stage one feeds stage two. MAD is robust to outliers (note the 100 in the list—it doesn't skew the result much).

