## Anomaly Score via Z-Score and Threshold

Combine z-score with a threshold to produce a binary anomaly flag or a severity score. Here's a program that computes z-scores and flags any value with |z| > 2 as an anomaly:

```scheme
;; Compute z-scores and flag anomalies (|z| > 2).
;; Returns list of (value z-score anomaly?) tuples.
(define (flag-anomalies series)
  (if (< (length series) 3)
      (map (lambda (x) (list x 'nan #f)) series)
      (let* ((mean (apply / (list (apply + series) (length series))))
             (sq-diffs (map (lambda (x) (expt (- x mean) 2)) series))
             (variance (apply / (list (apply + sq-diffs) (length series))))
             (stddev (sqrt variance)))
        (if (= stddev 0)
            (map (lambda (x) (list x 0 #f)) series)
            (map (lambda (x)
                   ;; Flag on the FULL-PRECISION z, report a rounded one. A value
                   ;; sitting a hair either side of 2 must not have its verdict
                   ;; decided by the display rounding.
                   (let ((z (/ (- x mean) stddev)))
                     (list x (math/round z 2) (> (abs z) 2))))
                 series)))))

(flag-anomalies '(10 12 14 16 18 50))
;; => ((10 -0.73 #f)
;;     (12 -0.59 #f)
;;     (14 -0.44 #f)
;;     (16 -0.29 #f)
;;     (18 -0.15 #f)
;;     (50 2.2 #t))
```

Compute mean and stddev once, map the z-score and flag computation. The flag is a boolean: `#t` if |z| > 2. This is a *classification map*—turn numeric data into labeled data.

Notice that *every* ordinary value here has a negative z-score, including the ones above the middle of the list. That is the outlier's doing: 50 drags the mean up to 20, which is higher than five of the six values, so five of the six sit below it. A single extreme point moves the very yardstick you are measuring against. This is the standard objection to z-scores for outlier detection, and it is why the median-based MAD above is the more robust tool when you expect contamination.

